 majesty” weakly performs deference and respectful recognition toward Lear as king and father. Cordelia's following explanation confirms that honour is part of the relational stance, though it is secondary to the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Obey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public royal proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to declare how much they love him while distributing the kingdom. After refusing elaborate competitive praise, Cordelia gives this restrained answer directly to Lear, contrasting with her sisters' expansive declarations.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression makes no new undertaking. Its surrounding qualification invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond, and her subsequent words associate that bond with fitting duties, obedience, love, and honour. Lear does not accept the measured avowal as adequate: he asks her to amend it, questions whether her heart agrees, and then repudiates her. Cordelia nevertheless confirms that her heart does agree with her statement.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare's King Lear, especially the love-test in Act I, Scene I. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly establishes the represented avowing force and supplies later corroboration of Cordelia's affection. It does not provide vocal performance details, and the exact focal words alone do not specify any concrete future commitment.\"}"
        }
